      spring: "Cool, damp and then bursting — afternoons rising from about 6°C in March to 19°C by late May, the lake keeping spring chilly and frost lingering into April. In May, warblers and other songbirds pour into Point Pelee after crossing Lake Erie, in one of the greatest bird migrations in North America.",
      summer: "Hot, humid and long — afternoons near 27°C, nights around 17°C, and the longest, warmest growing season in Canada. Thunderstorms roll in off the lake on muggy afternoons. Field tomatoes ripen by the truckload, and the beaches of Point Pelee fill with swimmers.",
